index 98cfd4037445539d14d6742d045dedcbf611e787..2f14de0d2a7c048c375279023ceb1cc3b24f1d43 100644
--- a/crates/metal2vulkan-runtime/README.md
+++ b/crates/metal2vulkan-runtime/README.md
@@ -284,6 +284,60 @@ Calls Metal does not have, for a consumer that emulates a Metal device:
   answer `GatherAnswer::Pointer`. A device without host-pointer imports takes that fallback
   directly; pointer-capable devices keep their gather route where it works.
 
+- Set `DeviceOptions::cpu_shadow` (or `RawDevice::cpu_shadow`) before creating the device,
+  then use `HostMemory::with_cpu_shadow` and `CpuShadowSource` for CPU-authoritative,
+  ordinary file-backed memory on Unix. The option defaults to false; the runtime refuses a
+  shadow import on an unconfigured device. `Device::cpu_shadow_enabled` reports this immutable
+  mode. It selects paged-address shader variants even before the first imported resource or
+  pipeline, so later dynamic residency cannot change the shader address ABI.
+  This path imports neither a pointer nor a dma-buf into Vulkan.
+  The source returns owned readable/writable file descriptors and byte ranges, exactly once
+  per resource. Canonical file identity and file offset own the Vulkan shadow bytes, so
+  overlapping views use the same GPU storage. `contents` remains the caller's ordinary CPU
+  pointer. A deferred pointer is materialized by the caller before CPU dereferencing.
+
+  This mode serializes actual GPU batches across the device. After earlier GPU work completes
+  and the batch's live-resource/root-table identities are fixed, the runtime snapshots all
+  live shadow ranges into coherent mapped Vulkan storage. It copies only GPU-changed bytes
+  back to the retained ordinary files before exposing events, listeners, completion handlers,
+  or CPU decoding of GPU-generated indirect records. Concurrent CPU writes to other bytes
+  survive that scatter. Unsynchronized CPU and GPU writes to the same byte remain caller
+  races. A failed scatter poisons the shadow store and later shadow batches fail rather than
+  continue with stale memory. External GPU writers of canonical backing are not supported;
+  neither an external semaphore nor a source callback transfers ownership of such writes.
+
+  Every shader view has an immutable table of canonical 4 KiB page addresses. Each actual
+  memory word is translated, so vectors and unaligned accesses crossing noncontiguous pages
+  preserve the physical mapping; naturally aligned atomics use the canonical address too.
+  Sparse aliased bindings are not required. Contiguous fixed-function views use separate
+  ordinary `VkBuffer` aliases over canonical memory at a stable logical offset. Fragmented
+  views gather into Vulkan-owned scratch before a fixed-function command and merge its byte
+  deltas back afterward, preserving writes through other aliases. A driver whose buffer bind
+  alignment prevents a direct alias uses the same scratch route. Heap and buffer textures use
+  the existing shadow-image layouts and copies, with canonical-range hazard tracking.
+
+  Rebinding within this backend preserves the resource's Metal address and identity. A new
+  view is registered before publication; submitted work retains the previous file descriptors,
+  page table, alias buffers and canonical storage, while the existing rebind machinery replays
+  eligible commands waiting for their first submission. An uncommitted encoded use still
+  refuses a rebind, as on the ordinary no-copy path. The new pointer must retain its original
+  low 12 bits; transitions to another import backend are explicitly refused. Detaching and
+  attaching again keep the resource's identity; textures present stand-ins while detached,
+  then restore their own images and gather from the replacement backing. Backing, alias,
+  live-table and texture metadata are published together against first submission's capture of
+  both named pages and dynamically reached roots. Source callbacks and replacement allocation
+  happen before this publication gate; replay, notices and old-owner retirement happen after it.
+  No GPU wait is added to publication. Source pieces must
+  describe one continuous physical window
+  within each logical 4 KiB page, with matching logical and physical page offsets; arbitrary
+  rearrangement at those page boundaries is supported. The source is resolved once for each
+  new backing, never again during its live epochs.
+  Registration may overlap a running batch: it never changes existing canonical bytes and
+  newly registered ranges join the next snapshot. `Device::cpu_shadow_counts` reports actual
+  canonical allocations, attached views, completed snapshots/scatters and bytes copied.
+  Canonical GPU allocations cover at most 1 MiB of each file at a time, shared across views;
+  snapshots cover the union of live ranges, including views that cross allocation boundaries.
+
 [docs/API.md section 10](../../docs/API.md#10-accepted-differences) lists where the runtime
 answers otherwise than Metal on purpose.
 
